acceptance:
Authoritative head inside the gate step. In today's `gate()` (:212-219), `authoritative_head()` returning a SHA different from the observed head raises ValueError, which parks the action. After this task, a different SHA is a race: start nothing, write no advance record, queue no wake, record no retry; the next poll observes the new head. Every exception raised inside the gate step is a failure: `authoritative_head()` raising (the stale-binding refusal, `fm_binding head` exiting non-zero), `base_tip()` raising (`git rev-parse` through `self.command`, :98-100, called at :214) and `start_job` raising. These failures they use the T-190 retry machinery (`retry_due` and `branch_failure` in bin/lib/fm_autopilot_branches.py:28-41) with token `advance:<number>:<head>`, retrying at poll offsets 0, 1 and 3 and queuing one wake on the third failure with the error text. A success at that head deletes the `advance:` retry token. Fingerprint resets. A fingerprint change on the same head while the `advance:` token is counting or exhausted starts a fresh count with its own wake. `branch_failure` (bin/lib/fm_autopilot_branches.py:33-41) gains an optional keyword `variant=''`. When it is non-empty: the retry record stores `variant` next to `count` and `due_seq`; a stored record whose `variant` differs is treated as count 0 before incrementing; and the wake identity becomes `f'{kind}-{number}-{head}-{variant}'`, so `queue()` (bin/lib/fm_autopilot.py:164-170), which skips identities already in `data['wakes']`, does not suppress the second series. The advance path passes the first 12 characters of the fingerprint as `variant`, and `retry_due` takes the same variant check, so a differing variant is due at once. Callers that pass no variant (update, sync) keep their exact record shape and wake identity. The fingerprint never goes into the token, because `prune_branches` splits tokens on ':' into three parts. The existing `pull()` handler that turns any advance exception into an 'advancement needs reconciliation' attention (bin/lib/fm_autopilot.py about 366-370) stays for errors outside the gate step (sync, CI reads, evidence reads). Tests:
- a differing authoritative SHA gives no gate, no wake, no retry and no `actions` entry;
- a raising `authoritative_head` gives retry counts 1, 2, 3 at offsets 0, 1, 3 and exactly one wake naming the error;
- a raising `base_tip` (with `authoritative_head` returning the observed head) takes the same `advance:` retry path and does not queue an 'advancement needs reconciliation' attention;
- a later success clears the token;
- exhausting the count, then changing the fingerprint at the same head (a new CI conclusion) while it still raises, gives counts 1, 2, 3 again and a second, distinct wake;
- the update and sync retry records and wake identities are unchanged (the existing T-190 tests keep passing as written).
